"""
GLE / VARX FINANCIAL FORECASTING PIPELINE

======================================================================
0. THE ONE-PARAGRAPH SUMMARY
======================================================================
We observe a daily market rate. We want a PROBABILITY DISTRIBUTION of where
it will be in the future, not a single number. The model splits tomorrow's
log-return into two parts:

    tomorrow's return = (predictable part) + (unpredictable shock)

    predictable part : a weighted sum of (i) exponentially fading memory of
                       past returns and (ii) today's returns on related
                       markets (oil, gold, equities, volatility, rates, USD).
                       Weights come from a sparse linear regression.
    unpredictable shock : random, with a size that itself changes through
                       time (calm periods vs. turbulent periods) and with
                       fat tails (occasional very large moves).

Having fitted both parts, we simulate thousands of possible futures day by
day, and read the distribution (5th / 50th / 95th percentiles) off the
simulated paths. Finally we TEST, out of sample, whether the predictable part
really beats the naive forecast "tomorrow's return is zero".

======================================================================
1. PHYSICAL PICTURE
======================================================================
Imagine one particle (the exchange rate) being pushed around inside a huge
fluid (the global market). We can watch only the particle. Two quantities are
used to describe it:

    r(t)     = ln P(t) - ln P(t-1)                     (log-return)
    sigma(t) = std( r(t-w+1), ..., r(t) ),  w = VOL_WINDOW   (realized volatility)

(Log-returns are used because they add over time -- a 5-day return is the sum
of five daily returns -- and because they measure percentage change.)

Everything else in the fluid is either (i) watched and supplied as an outside
push x_j(t) (oil, gold, ...), or (ii) not watched and mathematically
eliminated. Eliminating unwatched degrees of freedom (Mori-Zwanzig projection)
leaves a Generalized Langevin Equation (GLE) for the watched variable:

    dr/dt = - Int_0^t K(t-s) r(s) ds  +  SUM_j Omega_j x_j(t)  +  eta(t)

    K(t-s)  memory kernel. The hidden variables relax over finite times, so
            the past of r still pushes on the present: r alone is
            non-Markovian (its future depends on more than its present value).
    Omega_j how strongly the outside push x_j moves r (linear response).
    eta(t)  the leftover random force from everything eliminated (noise).

Financial reading: K is "momentum / mean-reversion with a fading memory",
Omega_j is "how much a move in oil/gold/equities today is associated with a
move in INR tomorrow", eta is "news we cannot predict".

No equilibrium is assumed: no fluctuation-dissipation relation between K and
the noise, no detailed balance, no Markov property. The only structural
requirements are (a) weak stationarity of r(t) over the sample (tested by ADF:
the mean and variance of r do not drift) and (b) a conditional mean that is
linear in its regressors with constant coefficients over each estimation
window.

======================================================================
2. DISCRETE MODEL (one INR trading day = one step)
======================================================================
Replacing the integral by a sum over days gives a linear regression:

    r(t+1) = SUM_k beta_k s_k(t)  +  SUM_j Omega_j x_j(t)  +  eta(t)

Every regressor on the right is known at the close of day t, so this is a
strictly causal one-step predictor (no look-ahead).

The memory enters through EXPONENTIAL MODE STATES, one per half-life h:

    s_h(t) = lambda_h s_h(t-1) + r(t),     lambda_h = 2^(-1/h)

s_h(t) is an exponentially weighted sum of all past returns in which a return
h days old counts half as much as a fresh one. A handful of half-lives
(1, 3, 10, 30, 90 days) spans short to long memory. The implied memory kernel
on the lag-k return is therefore

    K_k = SUM_h beta_h lambda_h^k     (a sum of decaying exponentials)

i.e. a smooth, few-parameter alternative to estimating one free coefficient
per lag. It can represent both short-lived and slowly fading dependence with
only len(HALF_LIVES) coefficients.

Identifiability: from one trajectory, linear memory K and linear
autocorrelation of eta cannot be told apart; both appear as linear dependence
on the past. K is therefore the TOTAL linear temporal dependence, and eta is
what remains after all linear dependence on the past and on the drives has
been removed.

Stability: the mode states form a feedback loop (s depends on r, r depends on
s). The loop is stable if the spectral radius of
A = diag(lambda) + 1 beta^T is below 1; otherwise simulated paths would
explode. A guard rescales the mode slopes when the radius approaches 1.

======================================================================
3. ESTIMATION (sparse linear regression)
======================================================================
All coefficients beta = (mode slopes, Omega_1..Omega_J) are estimated jointly
by elastic net on standardized regressors:

    min_beta  (1/2n) sum_t w_t (y_t - b0 - Z_t beta)^2
              + alpha [ rho ||beta||_1 + (1-rho)/2 ||beta||_2^2 ]

    y = r(t+1);  Z = column-standardized regressors;  rho = l1_ratio;
    w_t = observation weights (see below).

Plain meaning: fit a line, but charge a price for large coefficients.
  * The L1 part charges per unit of |coefficient| and drives weak
    coefficients exactly to zero (variable selection).
  * The L2 part charges per squared coefficient and stabilizes groups of
    correlated regressors.
  * alpha is the overall price; rho splits it between L1 and L2.
Financial reason: daily FX returns are almost pure noise, so an unpenalized
regression would "find" spurious relations. The penalty keeps only effects
strong enough to survive the price.

Details:
* Standardization (zero mean, unit variance per column) makes the penalty
  independent of measurement units; reported coefficients are STANDARDIZED:
  beta_std = beta_raw * sd(regressor) = change in r(t+1) per one-sd change of
  the regressor.
* The surviving drives {j : Omega_j != 0} are those that statistically LEAD r
  by one day (a lead-lag association, not proof of structural causation);
  excluded regressors are absorbed into eta.
* (alpha, rho) are chosen by cross-validation on TimeSeriesSplit: every
  validation block lies strictly AFTER its training block, so the future is
  never used to tune the past. The alpha grid is built from the data (from the
  smallest alpha that zeroes every coefficient, downward in logarithmic steps),
  so it adapts to the scale of y.
* Weights. Noise is heteroskedastic (its size varies) and heavy-tailed, so the
  mean is estimated in two passes: pass 1 is unweighted; its residuals are fitted
  by GARCH(1,1)-t; pass 2 repeats the elastic net with the Student-t
  maximum-likelihood (IRLS) weights
      w_t = (nu+1) / ((nu-2+z_t^2) h_t),   z_t = eta_t / sqrt(h_t),
  normalized to mean 1. Intuition: observations taken in turbulent states
  (large h_t) or with extreme residuals (large z_t) are less informative about
  the mean and are given less influence. The same estimator is used for the
  production fit and for every walk-forward refit.

======================================================================
4. NOISE MODEL
======================================================================
The residual eta(t) is modelled as GARCH(1,1) with unit-variance Student-t
innovations:

    eta(t) = sqrt(h(t)) z(t),   z(t) ~ standardized Student-t(nu)
    h(t)   = omega + alpha eta(t-1)^2 + beta h(t-1)

h(t) is the conditional variance: today's noise intensity given the past.
  * alpha reacts to yesterday's shock (a big move raises tomorrow's variance).
  * beta carries yesterday's variance forward (turbulence persists).
  * alpha+beta < 1 is the per-day relaxation factor of variance back to its
    long-run level omega/(1-alpha-beta); alpha+beta -> 1 means shocks to
    variance almost never fade (near-integrated variance, IGARCH).
  * nu sets the tail weight: small nu = frequent extreme moves; nu -> infinity
    recovers the Gaussian.
Parameters are obtained by maximum likelihood. Financially this is "volatility
clustering": calm days follow calm days, wild days follow wild days.

======================================================================
5. FORWARD SIMULATION (Monte Carlo)
======================================================================
Simulated return at day t+h depends on its own simulated past (through the
mode states), through the evolving noise variance (which depends on the
previous simulated shock), and through future drive values. The distribution
of r(t+H) therefore has no closed form for H > 1 and is obtained by pushing
an ensemble of N sample paths through the full recursion, one day at a time:

    1. draw the noise variance h from the previous shock,
    2. draw a Student-t shock,
    3. predicted mean + shock = simulated return,
    4. update every path's mode states with that return.

Empirical quantiles across the ensemble approximate the predictive
distribution conditional on information at time T under the fitted dynamics.
Cumulative log-returns are path sums; prices are P(T) exp(cumulative return).
Future drives are drawn by sampling a historical block of drive values
(bootstrap), independently of the simulated INR path.

Scope: the ensemble represents uncertainty from noise and from future drive
values. Fitted coefficients are treated as exact (parameter uncertainty is not
propagated), so the bands are scenario ranges, not calibrated confidence
intervals.

======================================================================
6. EVALUATION
======================================================================
Question answered: "Does the model beat naive forecasts on data it has not
seen?" -- the only test that matters for forecasting.

Walk-forward (expanding window): at each refit, using only data before the
prediction date, the weighted elastic net (including its hyperparameters) is
re-fitted and then predicts the next REFIT_EVERY days one step ahead.

Mean accuracy is compared with two benchmarks: the zero forecast E[r]=0
(random walk without drift) and the expanding-mean forecast (random walk with
drift, using only past data). Reported: RMSE, MAE, out-of-sample
R2 = 1 - SSE_model/SSE_zero (positive = beats zero), and the one-sided
Clark-West (2007) test. Clark-West is used because each competitor NESTS its
benchmark (the bigger model contains the smaller as a special case); estimating
extra useless parameters inflates squared error, and a plain comparison would
unfairly favour the smaller model. The Clark-West loss differential is

    f_t = (y-b)^2 - [(y-a)^2 - (b-a)^2] = 2 (y-b)(a-b),

tested with a HAC (autocorrelation-robust) variance.

The predictive DENSITY (what the simulation actually produces) is evaluated
one step ahead out of sample: the probability integral transform (PIT) of each
outcome under its forecast distribution must be Uniform(0,1) if the density
is right (Kolmogorov-Smirnov test); 5%/95% tail coverage should be 0.05 each;
and the mean log score is compared with a constant-variance Gaussian.

Adequacy of truncations is tested on the standardized errors z: Ljung-Box
(remaining linear dependence), ARCH-LM (remaining volatility clustering), and
CUSUM (stability of the mean of z).

Multi-horizon and density-scoring stages additionally evaluate cumulative
5- and 21-day returns, with training targets purged so that overlapping
horizons never leak outcomes into training, and CRPS (a score for whole
distributions; lower is better).

======================================================================
INPUTS
======================================================================
  usd_inr.csv        date, price
  market_drives.csv  date, and <name>_r (daily log return) for each of
                     broad_usd, brent_oil, gold, vix, sp500, nikkei, us10y

USAGE
  python gle_pipeline.py
  python gle_pipeline.py --csv usd_inr.csv --market market_drives.csv --outdir results
  python gle_pipeline.py --demo            # synthetic data with known ground truth
  python gle_pipeline.py --horizon 60 --n-sims 5000 --start 2019-01-01 --end 2025-06-30
  python gle_pipeline.py --build-dashboard # three-asset dashboard

REQUIREMENTS  scikit-learn >= 1.7 (ElasticNetCV(alphas=<int>)), arch, statsmodels, scipy

OUTPUTS (--outdir)
  features.csv  residuals.csv  simulation_forecast.csv  evaluation_report.txt
  model.pkl     pipeline_summary.json
"""

